    authorLinkedIn: "https://linkedin.com/in/blue-ocean-hub",
    content: "\nA UK limited company combined with a multi-currency account is one of the more practical structures for a non-resident selling services internationally. The company provides the entity and the credibility; the account provides the banking. Solving only one of the two leaves you half-equipped.\n\n## Why a UK Limited Company\n\n- **Familiar and inexpensive.** Straightforward formation, no minimum share capital, and a structure clients and platforms recognise.\n- **Limited liability.** The company's debts are the company's, subject to the usual caveats.\n- **Contracting.** Some clients will not contract with an individual and will with a company.\n- **Perpetual existence.** The entity continues regardless of the founder.\n\n## The Trade-offs\n\nA UK company creates ongoing obligations: annual filings, accounts, and tax registrations, whether or not it trades. Banking is the harder half, because not every provider will onboard a non-resident director. And a UK company does not by itself move your personal tax residence.\n\n## Formation\n\nChoose the name, confirm the share structure, appoint the director, register with Companies House, register for the relevant taxes, and then solve banking. The first steps are fast and cheap; banking is where the time goes.\n\n## The Banking Problem\n\nA multi-currency account is the piece that makes the structure usable. Without it, a UK company is an entity with nowhere to receive or hold funds conveniently. Compare providers on whether they will onboard you at all, then on the FX margin, the receiving facilities, and the ongoing fees.\n\n## What to Compare in an Account\n\n- **Receiving.** Can it receive from the clients and platforms you actually use?\n- **Currency holding.** Can you hold dollars or euros, or is conversion forced?\n- **Conversion rate.** The margin, not the fee, is usually the larger cost.\n- **Integration.** Does it connect to the payment tools and platforms you use?\n- **Ongoing cost.** Monthly fees, minimum balances, and inactivity charges.\n\n## The Sequence That Works\n\nForm the company, obtain the tax registrations, then apply for the account with the company's documents in order. Applying for banking before the company exists, or with an incomplete document pack, is the usual source of delay.\n\n## Is It Right for You\n\nIt suits a founder selling to UK or European clients, using UK payment infrastructure, or wanting a recognisable entity quickly. It suits less well a founder whose market and residence are both elsewhere and who has no specific need of the UK.\n\nOur [UK company guide](/article/uk-ltd-company-setup-non-residents) covers the entity side, and our [Wise versus Payoneer comparison](/article/wise-business-vs-payoneer-for-agencies) covers the account options.\n"
