    postType: "article",
    title: "Deep Value Investing on the PSX: Applying Ben Graham's Margin of Safety to South Asian Equities",
    description: "Screening PSX stocks trading below Net Current Asset Value (NCAV) and low Price-to-Earnings ratios.",
    metaDescription: "Deep value investing on PSX. Benjamin Graham margin of safety screening, Net Current Asset Value NCAV stocks, and P/E ratios.",
    pubDate: "2026-09-30",
    author: "Blue Ocean Hub Editorial",
    category: "Investing",
    tags: ["value investing", "PSX", "Benjamin Graham", "investing", "NCAV"],
    readingTime: 12,
    schema: "Article",
    authorLinkedIn: "https://linkedin.com/in/blue-ocean-hub",
    content: "\nValue investing's central idea is simple: buy a business for less than it is worth, and let the gap close. Applying it to the Pakistan Stock Exchange requires more care than in deeper markets, because the accounting is uneven, the disclosure is inconsistent, and the price can stay below fair value for years.\n\n## The Margin of Safety\n\nThe concept is that you should be able to be wrong about part of your estimate and still not lose money. If you value a business at a hundred and buy at seventy, you have absorbed a thirty percent error. If you buy at ninety-eight, you have absorbed almost none. The discipline is not finding cheap stocks; it is refusing to buy expensive ones.\n\n## The Problem of Estimating Value Locally\n\nValuation requires reliable earnings and a reliable balance sheet. On the PSX, both need checking:\n\n- **Earnings quality.** Is the profit from the business, or from a one-off, a related-party transaction, or a revaluation?\n- **Accounting.** Are the statements consolidated, audited, and consistent year to year?\n- **Related-party exposure.** Does the business trade with entities the promoter also controls?\n- **Cash conversion.** Does the profit arrive as cash, or does it sit in receivables?\n\nA low multiple on unreliable earnings is not a bargain; it is an unresolved question.\n\n## The Behavioural Side\n\nValue investing is uncomfortable. You buy things that are out of favour, often while they are falling, and you hold them while the fall continues. The investors who fail at it usually fail behaviourally: they buy too early, sell too early, or abandon the approach after a year in which it underperformed.\n\n## The Local Specifics\n\n- **Liquidity.** A cheap stock that trades once a week is cheap for a reason, and exiting may cost more than the margin of safety.\n- **Currency.** A business whose earnings are in rupees and whose costs are in dollars is not the value it appears.\n- **Promoter behaviour.** The single most important local variable: whether the controlling shareholder treats minority holders as partners or as a funding source.\n- **Time.** The gap can take years to close, and the holding period must be funded accordingly.\n\n## A Practical Screen\n\n- Low price relative to earnings, cash flow, and asset value — all three, not one.\n- A balance sheet that can survive a bad year.\n- Cash generation that matches reported profit.\n- A promoter record that suggests the minority will be treated fairly.\n- Sufficient liquidity to build and exit a position.\n\n## The Honest Verdict\n\nValue investing works when the analysis is done properly and the investor can hold. On the PSX, both conditions are harder than in developed markets, which is why the returns to patience are larger for those who do the work. It is not a method for someone who wants a result this quarter.\n\nOur [dividend aristocrats guide](/article/psx-dividend-aristocrats-index-portfolio) covers the income-oriented version of the same discipline.\n"
